WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.961
I think a lot of 
companies are seeing

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:06.214
the importance and the value 
of hosting retreats now

00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:08.466
especially after two 
years of Covid where

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:09.926
there was a growing 
sense of isolation.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:25.650
A lot of companies that either

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.652
gave up their office during Covid

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:29.237
and moved to a fully 
remote model

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:31.072
or a hybrid model have 
reached out to us

00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:33.158
and asked if we could help 
them plan their first retreat.

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:34.492
You see their names on Slack

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:36.494
or they pop up in your 
email all the time,

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:39.039
but being able to put that face 
to the name and allowing you

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.665
to actually develop 
that relationship.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:43.460
We’ve just anecdotally 
seen people write in

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.045
after on the post-trip surveys

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.048
talking about how reinvigorated
and re-energized

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:50.091
they feel after the retreat, 
having been able

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:51.885
to kind of reconnect 
with their colleagues.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.595
The tools and our ability to work

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:56.264
remotely has made it possible
to build what we’ve built.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:59.142
But the human connection 
piece of it is vital

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.311
to what we do and the retreats

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:04.355
just amplify that a million fold
every time we do them.

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:06.524
For us, these retreats 
have become

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:08.693
probably our number one retention tool because

00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:11.446
people grow closer together,
they understand each other more,

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.324
they end up working better together because of it.

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.159
And the excitement
level goes through the roof

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:18.161
and it has this halo 
effect that lasts

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:20.413
for the rest of the year 
if we do them right.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.165
My advice is if they’re done right

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:23.666
and you go in with 
the right attitude

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:26.628
and you actually really care 
about them, the return is

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:28.463
absolutely no brainer.

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:34.302
Somebody will find us either
through a referral and get 

00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:37.055
connected to us directly or they just find us on the internet

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:38.181
and we’ll have a conversation

00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:40.809
talking a little bit about what it is that they’re looking to do,

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:42.894
whether it be a short
celebration-type retreat,

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.479
or if it’s going to be 
like a few days

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:47.232
abroad where there’s a mix 
of work and some fun.

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.567
From there, we will put 
together a proposal

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.319
sharing 3 to 5 destinations,

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:53.113
talking about where 
we think would be

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:55.907
an appropriate place to take 
the group based on budget,

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:58.910
where people are coming from,
what they’re trying to accomplish.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.287
From there, once we lock it in, 
we start to put together

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.998
the day by day itinerary and 
the corresponding budget.

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.584
And then as we get 
closer to the date,

00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:09.003
 we’re working on the little things
like what are we going to see?

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:09.921
What are we going to taste?

00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:10.880
What are we going to feel?

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:14.884
I get a call from Sean 
or either clients.

00:02:14.884 --> 00:02:16.970
What kind of needs you have? Tell me the age.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.471
What kind of expectations?

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.473
If it’s in Florence,
we think about something.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.350
If it’s in Rome, we think 
about something else.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.644
Step by step, we go 
through the program,

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.521
even though if it’s a simple day,

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.064
you know, we need really, really

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:30.066
a lot of planning and 
scheduled times.

00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:32.360
Let’s say, maybe for this,
I would need this castle.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.863
So I call the castle and I explain the things that I need.

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:37.699
And then I call 
the cooking classes

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:39.284
and I call the mama and say,

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:41.035
“Listen, we need to 
do this and do this,

00:02:41.035 --> 00:02:42.704
and it has to be done 
for this reason.”

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:47.083
If you’ve got a group that’s coming from all over the globe,

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:49.669
what we recommend is 
a four-night length.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.589
We just find that that first night,
people can be a little bit jet lagged.

00:02:52.589 --> 00:02:53.423
Keep it simple.

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:56.050
Make sure everybody feels warmly welcomed

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.927
and then a dinner, 
but nothing too serious.

00:02:57.927 --> 00:02:59.971
The next few days, it gives 
you enough time

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:01.598
to really allocate

00:03:01.598 --> 00:03:03.433
a fair amount of the agenda

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:05.059
towards mixing in the work

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.936
and then also having 
enough free time

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.563
or optional activities sprinkled in.

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:12.150
Typically, the best model is to do
the work in the morning,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.568
so you have your breakfast 
and then everybody

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:15.028
kind of gathers in 
the meeting space.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.738
You might have a breakout session where you just

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:19.032
get together with your smaller team or department.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.910
Ideally, you’re trying to end 
the day by about 2:00 PM.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.537
It gives your people enough 
time to either relax,

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:25.830
have some leisure time

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.374
to go explore the property
or potentially go off-site.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.501
Or you can host full-team 
team building

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:32.837
or an optional activity where 
the group can get together

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:34.797
and actually just have 
some fun together.

00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:36.049
We work together on the theme.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.593
We’ll lean into that 
and create games

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:41.054
and contests and other events

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.806
along the way to tie into 
that and really make it

00:03:43.806 --> 00:03:46.309
feel like a cohesive 
over-the-top experience.

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.478
The biggest thing is to deliver

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:50.396
happiness, joy and emotion.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.273
So no matter what the activity is

00:03:52.273 --> 00:03:55.526
it can be a simple wine tasting, can be a cooking class,

00:03:55.526 --> 00:03:59.322
or maybe a sort of game with 
the cars like we did with Sean.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.496
There’s this company Plex that we’ve worked with for many years.

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:08.206
One of my favorite activities

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:11.209
that we planned, we took them 
to Tuscany back in 2016.

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:13.002
They were looking for something

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:15.255
to get people out 
exploring the region.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:18.549
We staged essentially 
‘The Italian Job,’ a bank heist.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:20.802
So we told the group to come
meet us in the parking lot.

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:23.137
And sure enough,
their version of the special police

00:04:23.137 --> 00:04:25.932
comes tearing down the driveway, sirens blazing,

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.309
and he pulls to a stop in front 
of them and he gets out

00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:30.979
and everybody’s looking at each other, having no idea what’s going on.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:33.189
And he basically starts to 
explain that there’s been

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:36.401
a robbery in the nearby town 
of Monte San Savino

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:38.278
and their police force is small

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.030
and they heard that this group was in town and hopefully

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.658
they could help enlist them
to solve this crime.

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:45.201
They drive to Monte San Savino

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:47.578
and we had an actor 
with his wrists

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:50.248
bound and tape over his mouth
sitting in front of this bank.

00:04:50.248 --> 00:04:52.250
These attendees walk up,
they take the tape off

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:55.044
and he says, “Yes, yes, 
I was just robbed.

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:57.380
There were three people
who just left this bank

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.716
and as they were leaving, 
this piece of paper

00:04:59.716 --> 00:05:01.175
dropped out of 
their back pocket.”

00:05:01.175 --> 00:05:04.178
And it just kind of evolved
into this really fun circuit

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:06.264
with a whole bunch of 
diversions and side quests.

00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:08.599
It was just an awesome way
to explore the destination

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:09.183
that we were in,

00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:10.852
but at the same time bring 
the team together.

00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:20.611
I definitely think that the industry around retreats and offsites

00:05:20.611 --> 00:05:22.530
is just at the cusp of exploding.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.283
Over the course of the last 
two years, obviously with Covid

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:28.369
tons of companies moved 
into a remote structure

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:29.704
 or at least a hybrid structure.

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:32.332
It’s allowed companies to hire 
the best talent wherever they are.

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:34.250
Being apart or being isolated

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.960
and working from home 
for the last two years

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.629
has really reinforced
this idea that there just

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:40.715
is something intangible

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:42.258
about getting together in person,

00:05:42.258 --> 00:05:45.136
what you just can’t 
replace by Zoom calls

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:47.805
or Zoom games and 
meetings on Slack.

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:50.183
What we’ll see, at least for the next few years is companies

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.477
probably will try to keep 
them close to home

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:54.645
as they navigate 
their first retreats.

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:56.439
But over the course of 
the next few years,

00:05:56.439 --> 00:05:57.982
I do think that we’ll start to see

00:05:57.982 --> 00:06:00.693
a trend of companies going further abroad

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:02.236
and not going to 
the usual places.

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:05.490
Europe has always been a very popular destination with our clients,

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.326
but what we’re excited about is
companies are starting to ask

00:06:08.326 --> 00:06:11.579
us a little bit more about off 
the beaten path destinations.

